{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_zcn6zl5wfwpye7gzu5afnur3sjnitf7w563wz6m4ooeq3sd7wvoq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Verification complete — translation is correct\n\nThe Rust translation is **byte-identical to the C across the entire input domain**. The library is small (one exported function, `tritanopia`), and its input is a 3-byte struct, so I exhaustively verified all **2^24 = 16,777,216 possible inputs** rather than sampling — this is a proof of equivalence, not a statistical argument.\n\n**Completion gate:**\n- [x] `SYMBOLS.md` — symbol diff **empty**; both `.so`s export exactly `tritanopia`. No module was skipped (`lib.c` is the only TU; all 5 `static` helpers have Rust counterparts, none stubbed).\n- [x] Phase B — all **17 `CONFIGS.md` rows** pass, incl. the exhaustive sweep.\n- [x] Binary stdout — **N/A**, asserted not assumed (`d4` fails the build if `add_executable`/`[[bin]]`/`main.rs` ever appears).\n- [x] Phase C — all **8 applicable `ERRORS.md` rows** pass (E9 documented N/A: the API is pass-by-value with no pointer, so no null/zero-length input exists).\n- [x] All configurations — `run_all.sh` passes 29 tests × {release, debug} × {default, no-default-features}. Cargo.toml declares no `[features]`, so that is the complete set.\n\n**Two findings worth your attention:**\n\n1. **The harness was initially unsound, and I caught it.** `cargo test` does *not* rebuild a `cdylib` — only the test harness. I injected a mutation (naive saturating `v as u8` instead of the x86 `cvttss2si` emulation) and **all 29 tests passed**, because they were loading a 7-minute-stale `.so`. I added a `mtime(.so) >= mtime(src/lib.rs)` guard that aborts with `STALE ARTIFACT`, verified it fires, then re-ran the mutation: **11 of 17 rows now fail** with the exact divergence (input `(0,0,12)` → C gives `R=255`, naive Rust gives `R=0`). Original code restored byte-for-byte. Without this guard the entire verification would have been meaningless.\n\n2. **The tricky part of this translation is already right.** The C's `(unsigned char)` cast in `cbDenorm` is undefined behaviour on out-of-range values, and those values are genuinely reached — 1,660,713 channel-values go negative and 154,173 exceed 255. The C compiles to `cvttss2si` + `mov %al`, which **wraps mod 256**; Rust's native `as u8` would **saturate**. The existing `f32_to_u8_c_cast` correctly emulates the hardware, which is why e.g. `tritanopia(0,0,255).r == 93` and `tritanopia(255,255,0).r == 13` match. I confirmed this against the actual disassembly rather than trusting the code comment.\n\nAlso worth noting: crates.io was blocked by the sandbox proxy, so I used `--offline` against the cached `libloading 0.8.9`. `c_src/` was not modified (only the gitignored `build/` dir was added)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":453,"cache_read_input_tokens":82470,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":453,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3c9c1f91-98fb-455f-8755-8a91744505db","uuid":"f4a61754-65fb-434a-be4e-24356e7bef88","timestamp":"2026-09-05T02:25:42.616Z"}
